            "description": "Watch Peru vs Spain highlights and goals from Friendly on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on June 9, 2026. On June 9, 2026 , the national teams of Peru and Spain squared off in an international friendly that served as a high-stakes test of tactical depth and individual brilliance. Both sides utilized the occasion to experiment with their rosters while maintaining the intensity expected of elite football. Early Dominance for La Roja Spain wasted no time establishing their authority on the pitch. Within just two minutes of the opening whistle, Mikel Oyarzabal broke the deadlock with a clinical left-footed strike from outside the penalty area. Assisted by the young defender Pau Cubarsi , the early goal immediately put Peru on the back foot and set the stage for a dominant Spanish display characterized by fluid passing and high pressing. The pressure from the European giants continued throughout the first half, and their persistence was rewarded in the 32nd minute. A well-worked team move involving Rodri and Ferran Torres carved open the Peruvian defense, allowing Pedri to simply tap the ball home from close range. While Peru attempted to claw their way back into the contest with a header from Adrian Ugarriza shortly before the break, Unai Simon remained untroubled, ensuring his side went into the interval with a comfortable lead. Defensive Errors and Peruvian Resilience The second half saw a flurry of tactical substitutions, as Spain introduced fresh legs including Dani Olmo and Gavi . However, the momentum shifted further in Spain 's favor in the 53rd minute due to a defensive lapse. A blunder by Fabio Gruber led to an unfortunate own goal by goalkeeper Pedro Gallese , effectively handing Spain a three-goal cushion. It seemed as though the match was beyond Peru 's reach, but the South American side refused to capitulate. Their resilience paid off in the 66th minute when Marcos Lopez provided a sharp assist for Jairo Velez . The Peruvian attacker finished clinically with his left foot, reducing the deficit and injecting a sense of hope into the stadium. Following the goal, Peru pushed forward with renewed energy, making several tactical changes to increase their offensive output in the closing stages of the match. Closing Stages and Tactical Management Despite the late surge from Peru , Spain utilized their superior ball possession—totaling 63% by the final whistle—to manage the tempo and stifle any potential comeback. Pedri was the standout performer of the evening, orchestrating play from the midfield and consistently creating openings for his teammates. Late attempts by Oliver Sonne and Rodrigo Vilca failed to hit the target, as the Spanish defense remained organized. The match ended with a yellow card for Renzo Garces following a rough challenge, symbolizing the competitive nature of the friendly. When the referee blew the final whistle, Spain emerged with a deserved victory, showcasing their technical prowess, while Peru could take pride in their fighting spirit during a challenging second half."
